Subject: [PATCH] kbase: ask for the tiler OOM exception when the queue group has its handler

Purpose: with no BASE_CSF_TILER_OOM_EXCEPTION_FLAG on the queue group, kbase terminates the group
 when the tiler heap reaches its last chunk (max_chunks). With the flag it answers the event with
 a NULL chunk instead, and the firmware runs the TILER_OOM handler on the CS that registered it:
 incremental rendering, the same path panthor takes. The handler programs already exist (the
 device builds them) and every render pass already points the fragment CS at one with
 cs_set_exception_handler; only the flag was never requested. All subqueues of a PanVK queue
 already share one kbase group, which the firmware needs: the handler only reaches CSs of the
 group that hit the OOM.
 The flag and the handler are one decision: group create takes tiler_oom_handler, and the caller
 passes whether the handler programs exist. The log says when the exception was requested.
 This backend only speaks the 1.21 handshake, whose group-create layout always has csi_handlers.
 If a kernel refuses the flag, the group is created again without it (then, as before, at the
 priority asked for), so a phone that does not take it keeps its driver.
Source/Reference: zenithblue-oss/panvk-kbase-android (MIT scaffolding), jm-v7-wip 0161 and its
 notes; re-written for this port (which keeps panthor's heap operations), no code copied.
Generated against: PANVK_REV 58f2c55854bd48b18df756edb11f187af9cd2fde with kbase-group-priority-high
 applied first (patches-panvk files apply in name order).
Not shown yet: that it stops the heavy-load restart (ticket #23). Not done here: zenithblue's 098
 (heap operations kbase accepts), 043/102 (heap renewal) and 097: ticket #24, only if #23 still
 restarts.

diff -ruN a/src/panfrost/lib/kmod/kbase_kmod.c b/src/panfrost/lib/kmod/kbase_kmod.c
--- a/src/panfrost/lib/kmod/kbase_kmod.c	2026-11-09 15:53:59.010339191 +0000
+++ b/src/panfrost/lib/kmod/kbase_kmod.c	2026-11-09 22:09:35.960366981 +0000
@@ -2071,6 +2071,11 @@
    uint32_t features, padding;
 };
 
+/* csi_handlers: the group has a tiler out-of-memory handler. At the heap's last chunk kbase then
+ * answers with a NULL chunk and the firmware runs the handler on the CS that registered it
+ * (incremental rendering), instead of kbase terminating the group. */
+#define BASE_CSF_TILER_OOM_EXCEPTION_FLAG (1u << 0)
+
 union kbase_ioctl_cs_queue_group_create {
    struct {
       uint64_t tiler_mask;
@@ -2523,7 +2528,8 @@
 struct pan_kmod_kbase_csf_group *
 pan_kmod_kbase_csf_group_create(struct pan_kmod_dev *dev, unsigned nr_queues,
                                 uint64_t compute_mask, uint64_t fragment_mask,
-                                uint8_t max_compute, uint8_t max_fragment, uint8_t priority)
+                                uint8_t max_compute, uint8_t max_fragment, uint8_t priority,
+                                bool tiler_oom_handler)
 {
    struct kbase_kmod_dev *kdev = container_of(dev, struct kbase_kmod_dev, base);
    struct kbase_csf_dev *c = kdev->csfq;
@@ -2556,6 +2562,7 @@
    if (priority == PAN_KMOD_KBASE_CSF_PRIORITY_MEDIUM)
       priority = PAN_KMOD_KBASE_CSF_PRIORITY_HIGH;
 
+   bool oom = tiler_oom_handler;
    union kbase_ioctl_cs_queue_group_create gc;
    for (;;) {
       /* Built afresh each time: a failed ioctl may have written into the union. */
@@ -2569,10 +2576,22 @@
             .tiler_max = 1,
             .fragment_max = max_fragment,
             .compute_max = max_compute,
+            /* Never without the handler: the flag makes kbase hand the out-of-memory event to
+             * the firmware, which needs the handler programs to be there. This layout (1.21,
+             * the only one this backend speaks) always has the field. */
+            .csi_handlers = oom ? BASE_CSF_TILER_OOM_EXCEPTION_FLAG : 0,
          },
       };
       if (!pan_kmod_ioctl(dev->fd, KBASE_IOCTL_CS_QUEUE_GROUP_CREATE, &gc))
          break;
+      /* What the kernel may refuse goes one thing at a time, the newer request first: a kbase
+       * that doesn't take the OOM flag must not cost the driver its device either. */
+      if (oom) {
+         mesa_logw("kbase: queue group with the tiler OOM exception refused (err=%d), asking "
+                   "without it", errno);
+         oom = false;
+         continue;
+      }
       if (priority == requested) {
          mesa_loge("kbase: CS_QUEUE_GROUP_CREATE failed (err=%d)", errno);
          goto err_free_seq;
@@ -2582,6 +2601,9 @@
       priority = requested;
    }
    g->handle = gc.out.group_handle;
+   if (oom)
+      mesa_logi("kbase: queue group %u created with the tiler OOM exception requested",
+                (unsigned)g->handle);
 
    for (unsigned q = 0; q < nr_queues; q++) {
       struct kbase_csf_ring *r = &g->ring[q];
diff -ruN a/src/panfrost/lib/kmod/kbase_kmod.h b/src/panfrost/lib/kmod/kbase_kmod.h
